## 1. What is React Native?

```
Normal React (Web)          React Native (Mobile)
──────────────────          ─────────────────────
runs in a browser           runs on a phone
makes HTML elements         makes native UI elements
<div>, <p>, <button>        <View>, <Text>, <TouchableOpacity>
renders to a webpage        renders to iOS / Android
```

The key idea — **you write JavaScript/TypeScript, but the phone renders real native components.** Not a website wrapped in a browser. Actual native Android/iOS views.

---

## 2. TypeScript vs JavaScript

Your app uses TypeScript (`.tsx` files). The difference:

```ts
// JavaScript — no types, no safety
function greet(name) {
  return 'Hello ' + name;
}
greet(123); // works, but wrong — no warning

// TypeScript — types protect you
function greet(name: string): string {
  return 'Hello ' + name;
}
greet(123); // ❌ ERROR caught before app even runs
```

TypeScript is just JavaScript with a safety net. Every variable, function, and component prop has a declared type. That's why you saw those 50 "implicitly has any type" errors earlier — TypeScript was saying "I don't know what type this is, tell me."

