## 3. How React Native renders your screen

Everything on screen is a **component** — a function that returns UI:

```ts
// A component is just a function that returns UI
function MyCard() {
  return (
    <View style={{ padding: 16, backgroundColor: 'white' }}>
      <Text>Hello!</Text>
    </View>
  );
}
```

React Native's core building blocks:

```
View              → like a <div> — a container box
Text              → like a <p>  — shows text
TextInput         → like <input> — user can type
TouchableOpacity  → like <button> — tappable, fades on press
ScrollView        → scrollable container
FlatList          → efficient scrollable LIST of items
SafeAreaView      → stays away from notch/status bar
```

---

## 4. State — how your app remembers things

`useState` is how a component remembers values that can change:

```ts
const [username, setUsername] = useState('');
//     ↑ current value   ↑ function to change it   ↑ starting value

// When user types, we update state:
<TextInput
  value={username}              // shows current value
  onChangeText={t => setUsername(t)}  // updates it on every keystroke
/>
```

Every time state changes → React re-renders the component → screen updates. This is the entire mental model of React.

